With this new change it is possible to better extend the Mailer/Message class to be able to customize the properties that can be serializable.

For example:
```php
<?php
declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Mailer;

use Cake\Mailer\Message;

class CustomMessage extends Message {
    protected $serializableProperties = [
        'to', 'from', 'sender', 'replyTo', 'cc', 'bcc', 'subject',
        'returnPath', 'readReceipt', 'emailFormat', 'emailPattern', 'domain',
        'attachments', 'messageId', 'headers', 'appCharset', 'charset', 'headerCharset',
        'textMessage'
    ];
}
```

These can provide a wrapper around the corresponding user-specified variable (such as PROMPT_COMMAND) allowing for things like customised or [enhanced shell integration protocols](https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/Per_Bothner/specifications/blob/master/proposals/semantic-prompts.md).

This provides the same functionality as withdrawn PR #7077 but as a much simpler (and more flexible) change.

# Description

The idea is that when evaluating `PROMPT_XXX` we first check if `WRAP_PROMPT_XXX` is defined.  If so we evaluate it instead.  While `WRAP_PROMPT_XXX` can in principle return any string it wants, the intended use-case is for `WRAP_PROMPT_XXX` to call the user-preference `PROMPT_XXX` and then modify or wrap the result. This is useful for shell-integration and might have other uses.

Using `WRAP_PROMPT_XXX` functions allows users to customize the prompts and indicators in the documented way, without getting in the way of shell-integration or other UI protocols.

The iTerm2-style shell-integration `PRE_PROMPT_MARKER` and `POST_PROMPT_MARKER` strings are ignored if `WRAP_PROMPT_COMMAND` is defined.

This is how these variables can be used for Domterm:
```
let-env WRAP_PROMPT_COMMAND = if "DOMTERM" in (env).name {
    { $"\u001b]133;P;k=i\u0007(do $env.PROMPT_COMMAND)\u001b]133;B\u0007" }
  } else $env.PROMPT_COMMAND
let-env WRAP_PROMPT_COMMAND_RIGHT = if "DOMTERM" in (env).name {
    { $"\u001b]133;P;k=r\u0007(do $env.PROMPT_COMMAND_RIGHT)\u001b]133;B\u0007" }
  } else $env.PROMPT_COMMAND_RIGHT
let-env WRAP_PROMPT_INDICATOR = if "DOMTERM" in (env).name {
    { $"\u001b]133;P\u0007(do $env.PROMPT_INDICATOR)\u001b]133;B\u0007" }
  } else $env.PROMPT_INDICATOR
let-env WRAP_PROMPT_INDICATOR_VI_INSERT = if "DOMTERM" in (env).name {
    { $"\u001b]133;P\u0007(do $env.PROMPT_INDICATOR_VI_INSERT)\u001b]133;B\u0007" }
  } else $env.PROMPT_INDICATOR_VI_INSERT
let-env WRAP_PROMPT_INDICATOR_VI_NORMAL = if "DOMTERM" in (env).name {
    { $"\u001b]133;P\u0007(do $env.PROMPT_INDICATOR_VI_NORMAL)\u001b]133;B\u0007" }
  } else $env.PROMPT_INDICATOR_VI_NORMAL
let-env WRAP_PROMPT_MULTILINE_INDICATOR = if "DOMTERM" in (env).name {
    { $"\u001b]133;P;k=c\u0007(do $env.PROMPT_MULTILINE_INDICATOR)\u001b]133;B\u0007" }
  } else $env.PROMPT_MULTILINE_INDICATOR
```
The above enables the following features on DomTerm:
* Move cursor by clicking mouse.  This is simulated by the terminal sending Left/Right arrow keys.
* Command errors are indicated with a small red circle.  Hovering over it displays the error code.
* If you change the window width, it automatically adjusts the position of right prompts in the scroll-back buffer.  The prompt is hidden if there isn't enough space on the line. _Note_: While reedline adjusts the right-prompt position in the _current_ command, for previous commends this had to be handled by the terminal.
* "Folding": Little triangles that when clicked show or hide the output of a command.
* Right prompt and indicators are distinguished internally and visibly.

See [this old blog article](http://per.bothner.com/blog/2019/shell-integration-proposal/) for more about these features, with (non-Nu) screenshots.`

